WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.399
So I wanted to prove that all marketing is
the same thing.

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:07.609
No matter what you're selling, it was always
the same thing.

00:00:07.609 --> 00:00:09.380
And good marketing talked about the buyer.

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:11.700
And bad marketing talked about the seller.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:13.880
Make sense?

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.990
"I" versus "you."

00:00:16.990 --> 00:00:19.800
So to illustrate my point

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.422
I wanted to prove that what the homeless do

00:00:22.422 --> 00:00:23.790
and what Microsoft does

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:24.860
is the same thing.

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.820
So if you think about what the homeless do

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.337
there is advertising.

00:00:29.337 --> 00:00:31.900
They sit there with a little sign that says,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:36.589
"I'm homeless, I'm hungry, 
I've got six kids, I'm a veteran ..."

00:00:36.589 --> 00:00:37.699
trying to appeal to everybody.

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:39.789
That's called a little billboard.

00:00:39.789 --> 00:00:41.000
Microsoft has a billboard that says,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.539
"More memory, bigger screen ..." all the rest of it.

00:00:44.539 --> 00:00:47.589
More RAM, more ROM.

00:00:47.589 --> 00:00:50.050
And what the homeless are selling is goodwill.

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.600
So if you walk past a homeless person

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.350
and you put money in their cup, 
you feel good.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.409
If you give them nothing, 
you either feel nothing or feel bad.

00:00:57.409 --> 00:00:58.449
You paid for that feeling.

00:00:58.449 --> 00:00:59.859
There was an exchange of consideration.

00:00:59.859 --> 00:01:03.089
I sold you goodwill, you paid for it,
you got it.

00:01:03.089 --> 00:01:04.590
They're selling goodwill, 
that's what they're doing.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.320
They're selling the feeling of goodwill.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:10.000
So I found a homeless person who was
willing to help me

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.549
and her sign was pretty typical

00:01:11.549 --> 00:01:16.740
and I found out that she makes between twenty
and thirty dollars a day selling goodwill.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.310
And she works an eight to ten-hour day.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.640
Thirty dollars is considered a good day,
selling goodwill.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:25.729
And like I said, her sign was pretty typical.

00:01:25.729 --> 00:01:27.300
So I asked her if I could change her sign

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:28.900
and we did.

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:34.950
And the sign that I gave her,
she made forty dollars in two hours.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.900
The sign that I gave her said,

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:41.119
"If you only give once a month,
please think of me next time."

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:43.120
It wasn't about the seller,

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.450
it was about the giver.

00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:47.789
What are the reasons people say they don't give?

00:01:47.789 --> 00:01:50.780
How do I know that they're genuine,
I can't give to everyone.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:51.900
So I simply answered the questions.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:54.869
If you can only give once a month,
please think of me next time.

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:57.729
I know you can't give to everybody,
I'm legit.

00:01:57.729 --> 00:01:59.950
That's all.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.549
Now here's the point I was going to make.

00:02:02.549 --> 00:02:04.689
She could have made one hundred and fifty dollars
that day.

00:02:04.689 --> 00:02:08.170
But after she made her forty dollars in two hours,
she left.

00:02:08.170 --> 00:02:10.700
Because one of the reasons she's homeless,

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:14.300
or we could surmise that
one of the reasons she's homeless is

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:19.740
she's decided that she needs
twenty or thirty dollars to live per day.

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:22.130
So once she had it, she moved on.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:25.430
That's a narrative.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:28.670
So she'll stay there because she didn't sit longer.

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:29.810
That's a narrative.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:33.890
So there's a wonderful little trick you can
use with your narrative.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:38.670
Just add the word 'yet' at the end.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:39.940
I'm not famous,

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:40.610
yet.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.460
I'm not that talented,

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:43.870
yet.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.100
I'm not good at auditions, I need to get better
at auditions, I suck at auditions,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.640
for now.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.004
You can just add a couple of words of hope

00:02:53.004 --> 00:02:55.480
or opportunity,
or the potential for future,

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:00.260
or some sort of infinite component
that will profoundly change your narrative.

